-</div>
-<p class="example-end"></p></blockquote>
-<p class="Textbody">In other languages, the optative is allowed, but the appeal still cannot be a simple command. In <a class="Language" href="https://wals.info/languoid/lect/wals_code_stl" title="view language details">Santali</a> (<a class="Genus" href="https://wals.info/family/austroasiatic#munda" title="Munda">Munda</a>; <a class="Country" href="https://wals.info/country/IN" title="view languages for country">India</a>; data obtained with the help of Dmitrij Sitchinava), optatives are allowed for addressing the hearer and the effect is that of a mild suggestion.</p>
-<blockquote class="example"><p class="example-start">(11) <a class="Language" href="https://wals.info/languoid/lect/wals_code_stl" title="view language details">Santali</a> (<a class="Reference" href="https://wals.info/refdb/record/Bodding-1952" title="view reference details">Bodding 1952</a>: 73)</p>
-<div style="text-align:left">
-<table class="table table-hover">
-<tr>
-<td style="text-align:left;">
-<p class="Standard"><i>Mit’ </i></p>
-</td>
-<td style="text-align:left;">
-<p class="Standard"><i>taka-m </i></p>
-</td>
-<td style="text-align:left;">
-<p class="Standard"><i>em-k-iñ-a? </i></p>
-</td>
-</tr>
-<tr>
-<td style="text-align:left;">
