-<p class="Standard">good-<span class="T3">stat-imp</span>.3</p>
-</td>
-<td style="text-align:left;">
-<p class="Standard">my </p>
-</td>
-<td style="text-align:left;">
-<p class="Standard">son </p>
-</td>
-</tr>
-<tr>
-<td style="text-align:left;">
-</td>
-<td colspan="3" style="text-align:left;">
-<p class="Standard">‘May my son be all right!’ </p>
-</td>
-</tr>
-</table>
-</div>
-<p class="example-end"></p></blockquote>
-<p class="Textbody">In <a class="Language" href="https://wals.info/languoid/lect/wals_code_cle" title="view language details">Lealao Chinantec</a> (<a class="Family" href="https://wals.info/languoid/family/otomanguean" title="Oto-Manguean">Oto-Manguean</a>; Oaxaca, <a class="Country" href="https://wals.info/country/MX" title="view languages for country">Mexico</a>; <a class="Reference" href="https://wals.info/refdb/record/Rupp-1989" title="view reference details">Rupp 1989</a>: 95) and in <a class="Language" href="https://wals.info/languoid/lect/wals_code_lez" title="view language details">Lezgian</a> (<a class="Family" href="https://wals.info/languoid/family/nakhdaghestanian" title="Nakh-Daghestanian">Nakh-Daghestanian</a>; <a class="Reference" href="https://wals.info/refdb/record/Haspelmath-1993" title="view reference details">Haspelmath 1993</a>: 151-152), on the other hand, it is the optative third person that does double duty. </p>
-<h2 class="heading-1">
-<a name="4._Further_issues"></a>4. Further issues</h2>
-<p class="Textbody">There are languages with more than one morphological optative, and in that case they will have different meanings. Thus in <a class="Language" href="https://wals.info/languoid/lect/wals_code_abk" title="view language details">Abkhaz</a> (<a class="Family" href="https://wals.info/languoid/family/northwestcaucasian" title="Northwest Caucasian">Northwest Caucasian</a>), one of the optatives, glossed as <span class="T3">opt1</span> here (<a class="Reference" href="https://wals.info/refdb/record/Hewitt-1979" title="view reference details">Hewitt's (1979</a>: 198) "subjunctive"), is used to curse and to bless, while the other one, the <span class="T3">opt2</span>, is used to express a wish or a dream of the speaker.</p>
-<blockquote class="example"><p class="example-start">(13) <a class="Language" href="https://wals.info/languoid/lect/wals_code_abk" title="view language details">Abkhaz</a> (<a class="Reference" href="https://wals.info/refdb/record/Hewitt-1979" title="view reference details">Hewitt 1979</a>: 198; Hewitt, p.c.) </p>
-<div style="text-align:left">
-<table class="table table-hover">
-<tr>
-<td style="text-align:left;">
-<p class="Standard">a. </p>
-</td>
-<td style="text-align:left;">
-<p class="Standard"><i>Bzia </i></p>
-</td>
-<td colspan="2" style="text-align:left;">
-<p class="Standard"><i>wy-ba-aait'! </i></p>
-</td>
-</tr>
-<tr>
-<td style="text-align:left;">
-</td>
-<td style="text-align:left;">
-<p class="Standard">good </p>
-</td>
-<td colspan="2" style="text-align:left;">
-<p class="Standard">you-see-<span class="T3"><span class="hint--bottom" data-hint="optative">opt</span></span>1</p>
-</td>
-</tr>
-<tr>
-<td style="text-align:left;">
-</td>
-<td colspan="3" style="text-align:left;">
-<p class="Standard">‘Greetings!’ (lit. ‘May you see something good!’) </p>
-</td>
-</tr>
-<tr>
-<td style="text-align:left;">
-<p class="Standard">b. </p>
-</td>
-<td style="text-align:left;">
-<p class="Standard"><i>Ma </i></p>
-</td>
