-<p class="Firstlineindent">In the third type, the second singular prohibitive employs <b>a sentential negative that is used in declaratives or, if the notion of indicative is relevant, in indicative declaratives, but the verbal construction is not the one found in second singular imperatives</b>. In <a class="Language" href="https://wals.info/languoid/lect/wals_code_spa" title="view language details">Spanish</a> the sentential negation for both negative indicative declaratives and prohibitives is the marker <i>no</i>. However, the prohibitive verb is not an imperative, but a subjunctive.</p>
-<blockquote class="example"><p class="example-start">(3) <a class="Language" href="https://wals.info/languoid/lect/wals_code_spa" title="view language details">Spanish</a> </p>
