-<p class="Textbody">Several complications occur. First, the prohibitive negation may be partially identical to an (indicative) declarative negation. In <a class="Language" href="https://wals.info/languoid/lect/wals_code_ykm" title="view language details">Yakoma</a> (Adamawa-Ubangian; <a class="Country" href="https://wals.info/country/CF" title="view languages for country">Central African Republic</a>) there are four declarative negations and two prohibitive ones. The latter consist of two parts, and the second part is in each case identical to a declarative negation. Negative markers in <a class="Language" href="https://wals.info/languoid/lect/wals_code_ykm" title="view language details">Yakoma</a> are given in example (5). </p>
-<blockquote class="example"><p class="example-start">(5) <a class="Language" href="https://wals.info/languoid/lect/wals_code_ykm" title="view language details">Yakoma</a> (<a class="Reference" href="https://wals.info/refdb/record/Boyeldieu-1995" title="view reference details">Boyeldieu 1995</a>: 131-132) </p>
